This uses the same Postgres and `node-postgres` assumptions as the cleanup job, with an Express-style handler. I've added two columns (`lock_id`, `locked_at`) to the earlier table so stale `in_progress` rows can be taken over safely.

## Table definition

```sql
CREATE TABLE idempotency_keys (
  user_id       text        NOT NULL,
  key           text        NOT NULL,
  request_hash  text        NOT NULL,
  status        text        NOT NULL CHECK (status IN ('in_progress', 'completed')),
  lock_id       uuid,        -- identifies which request currently owns the key
  locked_at     timestamptz, -- when that request claimed it (for stale detection)
  response_code int,
  response_body jsonb,
  created_at    timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now(),
  PRIMARY KEY (user_id, key),
  CHECK (status <> 'in_progress' OR (lock_id IS NOT NULL AND locked_at IS NOT NULL)),
  CHECK (status <> 'completed'   OR response_code IS NOT NULL)
);

CREATE INDEX idempotency_keys_created_at_idx ON idempotency_keys (created_at);
```

If you already created the earlier version, migrate it with:

```sql
