## The policy

| Level | Who is paged | When |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | Primary on-call | Immediately |
| 2 | Backup on-call | After 10 minutes unacknowledged |
| 3 (optional) | Engineering manager | After a further 10 minutes unacknowledged |

The whole chain repeats once if nobody acknowledges. Only **acknowledging** stops escalation. Viewing the alert doesn't.

## PagerDuty (Terraform)

```hcl
variable "primary_user_ids" { type = list(string) }  # same order as rota config
variable "manager_user_id"  { type = string }

resource "pagerduty_schedule" "primary" {
  name      = "On-call: primary"
  time_zone = "Europe/London"

  layer {
    name                         = "Primary rotation"
    start                        = "2025-07-07T10:00:00+01:00"
    rotation_virtual_start       = "2025-07-07T10:00:00+01:00"
    rotation_turn_length_seconds = 604800  # weekly
    users                        = var.primary_user_ids
  }

  # rota.json is applied as overrides (or replaces this layer) by the sync step
  lifecycle { ignore_changes = [layer] }
}

resource "pagerduty_schedule" "backup" {
  name      = "On-call: backup"
  time_zone = "Europe/London"

  layer {
    name                         = "Backup rotation"
    start                        = "2025-07-07T10:00:00+01:00"
    # Offset by one week so backup = next week's primary, matching rota.py
    rotation_virtual_start       = "2025-07-14T10:00:00+01:00"
    rotation_turn_length_seconds = 604800
    users                        = var.primary_user_ids
  }

  lifecycle { ignore_changes = [layer] }
}

resource "pagerduty_escalation_policy" "oncall" {
  name       = "On-call escalation"
  num_loops  = 1  # repeat the whole chain once if nobody acks

  rule {
    escalation_delay_in_minutes = 10
    target {
      type = "schedule_reference"
      id   = pagerduty_schedule.primary.id
    }
  }

  rule {
    escalation_delay_in_minutes = 10
    target {
      type = "schedule_reference"
      id   = pagerduty_schedule.backup.id
    }
  }

  # Optional level 3: delete this block if you don't want a manager in the chain
  rule {
    escalation_delay_in_minutes = 10
    target {
      type = "user_reference"
      id   = var.manager_user_id
    }
  }
}

resource "pagerduty_service" "main" {
  name              = "Production"
  escalation_policy = pagerduty_escalation_policy.oncall.id
  alert_creation    = "create_alerts_and_incidents"
  # Without this, low-urgency incidents can wait instead of paging
  incident_urgency_rule {
    type    = "constant"
    urgency = "high"
  }
}
```

## Things to check

- **The 10 minutes should not be silent for the primary.** Escalation only decides when the backup gets involved. Each person's own notification rules decide how hard the primary is chased in that window. If the two missed incidents were caused by a muted phone or a push notification that never arrived, a backup will catch it, but only after 10 minutes. A sensible setup per engineer is push immediately, a phone call at 1-2 minutes, and another call at 5. You can set these with `pagerduty_user_notification_rule`, or have each engineer set them in their profile.
- **Test it before you rely on it.** Trigger a test incident on the service, leave it unacknowledged, and confirm the backup is paged at 10 minutes. Do this once with the real schedules, since schedule or override mistakes usually show up here.
- **Base layers and overrides.** The schedules above rotate in the same order as the config but ignore leave. The sync step is what makes them follow `rota.json`, including unavailability. Until that exists, anyone on leave needs a manual override.
- **The level 3 manager rule is my addition.** It covers the case where both engineers miss the page. Remove it if you don't want it.

